    "description": "If you want electric for concrete, this is the one to look at, with a caveat on flow. Greenworks lists the GPW3001 at 3,000 PSI, and its listings give 1.1 GPM at that pressure and 2.0 GPM only at 100 PSI, which is why the headline numbers on electric washers can mislead. It uses a brushless motor that senses which nozzle is fitted, a 35-foot GFCI cord and a 25-foot steel-braided hose, and Greenworks covers it with a three-year limited tool warranty and a ten-year warranty on the brushless motor. That output will clean a patio or a short driveway, especially with a 12 to 14-inch surface cleaner, but it will be slower than any gas pick here. A vertical version, the GPW3000V, is also sold. For our detailed take on whether an electric unit can do the job, see our guide to electric washers on concrete.",
    "pros": [
      "3,000 PSI, per Greenworks",
      "Three-year tool and ten-year brushless motor warranty, per Greenworks",
      "Quiet, no fuel or oil to manage",
      "35-foot GFCI cord and steel-braided hose"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "About 1.1 GPM at rated pressure, so slower on large areas",
      "Best with a small 12 to 14-inch surface cleaner",
      "Two model numbers (GPW3001 and GPW3000V) cause confusion"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The Greenworks Pro GPW3001 is the strongest electric choice for patios and short driveways, at 3,000 PSI with a ten-year motor warranty, per Greenworks, but expect slower work than gas."
  },
  {
    "id": "ryobi-ry142300",
    "name": "Ryobi 2300 PSI Brushless Electric (RY142300)",
    "badge": "Best for Small Patios",
    "category": "Electric, 2,300 PSI, 1.2 GPM, Brushless",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "description": "For a small patio, a front walk and steps, a compact electric washer is enough, and Ryobi's RY142300 is a sensible one. Ryobi lists 2,300 PSI and 1.2 GPM from a 13-amp brushless motor and an axial cam pump with bypass, with a 35-foot GFCI cord, a 25-foot hose, 12-inch wheels, a one-gallon soap tank and a weight of 49 pounds, under a three-year limited warranty. That is not enough output for a dirty two-car driveway in one comfortable session, but it handles light grime, mildew and a pre-sealing rinse on smaller areas, especially with a 12-inch surface cleaner. It is also far quieter than gas and needs no winter fuel treatment. Ryobi's site lists one variant, the RY142300VNM, as discontinued, so check which version is in stock before you buy.",
    "pros": [
      "2,300 PSI and 1.2 GPM, per Ryobi",
      "13-amp brushless motor and three-year limited warranty, per Ryobi",
      "Light enough at 49 lb to carry to a patio",
      "Quiet, with no fuel or oil"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Too little flow for large or very dirty driveways",
      "Pairs only with a small surface cleaner",
      "One variant listed as discontinued by Ryobi"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "For small patios and walks, the Ryobi RY142300 offers 2,300 PSI and 1.2 GPM from a brushless motor with a three-year warranty, per Ryobi, at a low price."
  }
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Pressure Washers for Concrete",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-pressure-washer-for-concrete/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the best pressure washer for concrete?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For most driveways, the Simpson PowerShot PS3228, listed at 3,300 PSI and 2.5 GPM with a Honda GX200 and AAA triplex pump. On a budget, the Westinghouse WPX3200 is rated 3,200 PSI and 2.5 GPM. For large areas, the DeWalt DXPW4440 is rated 4,400 PSI and 4.0 GPM."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What PSI do you need to clean concrete?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Around 3,000 PSI is a good target for driveways, paired with 2.5 GPM or more of flow. Lower-pressure electric washers can clean patios and walks, more slowly, especially with a small surface cleaner and detergent. More pressure than you need increases the risk of etching."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is a gas or electric pressure washer better for concrete?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Gas, for most driveways. Gas washers here offer roughly twice the flow of electrics at higher pressure, so they finish faster and run larger surface cleaners. Electric makes sense for patios, walks and small areas, or where quiet, low-maintenance operation matters more than speed."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Do I need a surface cleaner for concrete?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Not strictly, but it is the biggest upgrade you can make. A rotary surface cleaner cleans an even path without wand stripes, works faster and keeps spray contained. Size it to the washer: around 15 to 16 inches for typical gas washers and 12 to 14 for electrics."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can a pressure washer damage concrete?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes, with narrow tips, too much pressure, or lingering in one spot. Older, soft or exposed-aggregate concrete is most at risk. Use wide tips or a surface cleaner, keep moving, test a hidden area first, and avoid washing new concrete before it has cured."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Will a pressure washer remove oil stains from concrete?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Not on its own. Oil soaks into concrete's pores, so treat stains with a degreaser or poultice first, let it dwell, then wash. Old or deep oil stains may lighten rather than disappear completely."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How often should you pressure wash a driveway?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Many homeowners clean once a year, often in spring or before sealing. Shady, damp driveways that grow mildew or algae may need it more often. Sealing after cleaning makes future cleaning easier and reduces how deeply stains soak in."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(Astro.url.pathname),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: authorName(Astro.url.pathname),
    section: "Home Improvement",
    tags: [
      "best pressure washer for concrete",
      "pressure washer for driveway",
      "best pressure washer for cleaning concrete",
      "best pressure washer for driveway",
      "concrete pressure washer"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <div class="absolute inset-0 opacity-10" style="background-image: radial-gradient(circle at 20% 20%, #38BDF8 0, transparent 45%), radial-gradient(circle at 80% 60%, #FF4500 0, transparent 40%);"></div>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex justify-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-[#FF4500]/20 text-[#FF4500] text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest border border-[#FF4500]/30">
            Buyer's Guide
          </span>
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500/10 text-green-400 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest border border-green-500/20">
            Buyer's Guide
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          Best Pressure Washers <span class="text-[#38BDF8]">for Concrete</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-8 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
          Seven washers matched to driveways, patios and big slabs, compared on the flow, pressure and pump that decide how fast and evenly concrete comes clean.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
          <a
            href="#simpson-powershot-ps3228"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-[#FF4500] hover:bg-[#E63E00] text-white font-black rounded-xl shadow-lg transition-all no-underline"
          >
            See Our #1 Pick
          </a>
          <a
            href="#top-picks"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white font-bold rounded-xl border border-white/10 no-underline"
          >
            See the Picks
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6 border-b border-gray-100 pb-8">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <AuthorAvatar size={48} />
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Alex Rivers</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest">Home Improvement Editor</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">{dates.display}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="11.2k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#how-we-picked" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. How We Picked</a>
          <a href="#psi-gpm" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. PSI, GPM and Cleaning Units</a>
          <a href="#top-picks" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Our 7 Picks</a>
          <a href="#surface-cleaner" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. Pairing a Surface Cleaner</a>
          <a href="#gas-vs-electric" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Gas or Electric</a>
          <a href="#pump" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Axial Cam vs Triplex</a>
          <a href="#technique" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Cleaning Without Damage</a>
          <a href="#stains" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Oil, Rust and Organic Stains</a>
          <a href="#sealing-after" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. Drying and Sealing</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">10. Common Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">11. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          The best pressure washer for concrete is a gas machine around 3,000 to 3,300 PSI and 2.5 GPM or more, paired with a 15 to 16-inch surface cleaner. Flow matters as much as pressure on a driveway, a triplex pump earns its price if you clean often, and an electric washer is enough only for patios and short walks.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="how-we-picked" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How We Picked
          </h2>

          <p>
            We did not run these machines on a test slab for this guide. We compared the specifications manufacturers and retailers publish: rated pressure, rated flow, engine or motor, pump type, wheels, hose and warranty. Where sources disagreed on a figure, such as engine size or hose length, we say so on the product card. The criteria are the ones that matter on concrete specifically, which is a narrower question than which pressure washer is best overall.
          </p>

          <p>
            Concrete is the job that separates pressure washers. A deck or a car needs gentle pressure and modest flow. A driveway needs enough flow to rinse loosened dirt away quickly and, ideally, enough output to drive a rotary surface cleaner, which is the attachment that turns a long, streaky job into an even one. That is why most of our picks are gas machines around 3,000 to 3,300 PSI and 2.4 to 2.7 GPM, with one heavy machine for large areas and two electrics for smaller ones.
          </p>

          <p>
            We weighted pump type and wheels more than we would for a general-purpose washer. Concrete sessions are long, and a triplex pump tolerates long run times better than the axial cam pumps on most budget machines. Pneumatic or never-flat wheels matter when you are dragging a 60-pound washer down a cracked driveway. For the broader market, including washers for siding, cars and decks, see our main <a href="/best-pressure-washer/">pressure washer guide</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            Every pick was listed for sale when we checked, but model numbers in this category change often and several brands sell near-identical machines under different part numbers. Check the PSI and GPM printed on the box against the numbers here before you buy.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="psi-gpm" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. PSI, GPM and Cleaning Units for Concrete
          </h2>

          <p>
            Pressure, measured in PSI, is what lifts dirt off the surface. Flow, measured in gallons per minute, is what carries it away and determines how fast you can move. On concrete, flow matters at least as much as pressure, because you are rinsing a large area rather than blasting one spot. Our guide to <a href="/what-psi-pressure-washer-do-i-need/">what PSI you need</a> puts concrete at around 3,000 PSI, the top of the household range, and that is a good target for a driveway.
          </p>

          <p>
            A common way to compare machines is cleaning units, which is simply PSI multiplied by GPM. The Simpson PowerShot at 3,300 PSI and 2.5 GPM works out to about 8,250 cleaning units, the Generac SpeedWash at 3,200 PSI and 2.7 GPM to about 8,640, and the DeWalt DXPW4440 at 4,400 PSI and 4.0 GPM to about 17,600. An electric washer listed at 3,000 PSI and 1.1 GPM comes to about 3,300. The figure is a rough guide rather than a guarantee, but it explains why a large gas washer finishes a driveway in a fraction of the time of an electric one.
          </p>

          <p>
            Be careful with electric ratings. Many list a peak PSI and a peak GPM that do not occur at the same time; the Greenworks listing, for example, gives 1.1 GPM at its rated 3,000 PSI and 2.0 GPM only at 100 PSI. Our explainer on <a href="/pressure-washer-psi-vs-gpm/">PSI versus GPM</a> covers how to read these numbers. When comparing, look for flow at rated pressure.
          </p>

