        <div id="surface-cleaner" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Pairing a Surface Cleaner
          </h2>

          <p>
            If you clean concrete more than once, buy a rotary surface cleaner. It is a round deck with a spinning bar and two or more nozzles that hovers a fixed distance above the ground, so it cleans an even path without the stripes a wand leaves. It is faster, it keeps spray off your legs and the house, and it is the single biggest upgrade you can make for driveways and patios. Our <a href="/best-surface-cleaner-for-pressure-washer/">surface cleaner guide</a> covers specific models.
          </p>

          <p>
            Size the cleaner to the washer, not to the driveway. Our <a href="/what-size-surface-cleaner-do-i-need/">sizing guide</a> matches roughly 12 to 14-inch decks to electric washers around 1,500 to 2,000 PSI and 1.5 to 2.0 GPM, 15 to 16-inch decks to typical homeowner gas washers at 2,800 to 3,400 PSI and 2.3 to 3.0 GPM, 17 to 18-inch decks to larger washers at 3,200 to 4,000 PSI and 3.0 to 4.0 GPM, and 20-inch decks and up to machines with 4.0 GPM or more. A deck that is too large for the washer's flow spins slowly or not at all and leaves swirls.
          </p>

          <p>
            On that basis, the PowerShot, SpeedWash, WPX3200 and MegaShot all suit a 15 to 16-inch cleaner, the DeWalt suits an 18 to 20-inch one, and the two electrics suit a 12 to 14-inch deck. Check the connection too: most gas washers use a 3/8-inch quick connector at the wand, and some cleaners need an adapter. If the result shows stripes or rings, our guide to a <a href="/surface-cleaner-leaving-streaks/">surface cleaner leaving streaks</a> walks through the causes.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Buy the washer for flow, then buy the surface cleaner for the washer.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">A 15 to 16-inch deck on a 2.5 GPM gas washer is the sweet spot for most homeowner driveways. Bigger decks need more flow, not more pressure.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="gas-vs-electric" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Gas or Electric for Concrete
          </h2>

          <p>
            For a typical two-car driveway, gas wins. The gas picks here deliver roughly twice the flow of the electrics at higher pressure, which means faster rinsing, a bigger surface cleaner and fewer passes. Our comparison of <a href="/electric-vs-gas-pressure-washer/">electric and gas pressure washers</a> covers the full trade-off, but on concrete specifically the gap is wide.
          </p>

          <p>
            Electric still makes sense in some cases. If your concrete is a patio, a short walk and some steps, an electric washer with a small surface cleaner will do the job quietly, without fuel, oil or a pull cord, and with far less maintenance over the winter. It is also the only practical choice where gas engines are restricted or noise is a concern. Our guide to whether <a href="/can-an-electric-pressure-washer-clean-concrete/">an electric pressure washer can clean concrete</a> goes into what to expect.
          </p>

          <p>
            The time difference is the cost of electric. On a heavily soiled driveway with oil spots and years of grime, an electric washer can take two or three times as long and may still leave some staining that a gas machine would lift. If that is your situation and you only need to do it once, renting a gas washer and a surface cleaner for a day can be cheaper than buying the larger machine.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whichever you choose, use a wide tip or a surface cleaner on concrete. The 0-degree tip and even the 15-degree tip can etch and scar softer or older concrete, especially at gas-machine pressures. Our explainer on <a href="/pressure-washer-nozzle-colors-explained/">nozzle colors</a> covers which tip does what.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="pump" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Pumps: Axial Cam vs Triplex
          </h2>

          <p>
            The pump is the part that does the work and the part most likely to fail on a budget machine. Most homeowner washers, including the SpeedWash, WPX3200 and electrics here, use an axial cam pump. It is compact, inexpensive and fine for occasional use, which for most people means a few sessions a year. Generac says its brass-head version lasts up to twice as long as aluminum-head pumps.
          </p>

          <p>
            Triplex pumps, like the AAA units on the Simpson PowerShot and the DeWalt, use three plungers driven by a crankshaft. They run cooler and slower for the same output and are generally rebuildable, which is why they appear on professional machines. On concrete, where you might run the washer for an hour or more at a time, that durability is worth paying for if you clean regularly.
          </p>

          <p>
            Two habits protect any pump. First, never let the washer run for long with the trigger released; water recirculates in the pump and heats up, which damages seals. Our guide to <a href="/how-long-can-a-pressure-washer-run-without-water/">running a pressure washer without water</a> explains the limits. Second, make sure your water supply can keep up: a washer drawing 2.5 to 4.0 GPM needs a hose and spigot that deliver at least that much. Our <a href="/pressure-washer-water-supply-requirements/">water supply guide</a> covers how to check.
          </p>

          <p>
            At the end of the season, winterize the pump with a pump protector or by running it dry as the manual directs. A cracked pump from frozen water is one of the most common and least necessary failures. Our <a href="/how-to-winterize-a-pressure-washer/">winterizing guide</a> walks through it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="technique" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. How to Clean Concrete Without Damage
          </h2>

          <p>
            Start by sweeping the area and moving anything that could be hit by spray. Pre-wet the concrete and nearby plants, then apply a concrete or degreasing detergent through the soap nozzle or the washer's soap system and let it dwell for the time the label specifies, without letting it dry. Detergent does much of the work on organic staining and grease, so you need less pressure.
          </p>

          <p>
            With a surface cleaner, work in straight, slightly overlapping passes at a steady walking pace, moving in the direction the water drains so dirty water runs onto areas you have not cleaned yet. With a wand, keep the tip at a consistent distance and angle and use a sweeping motion rather than holding it in one place. Lingering is what etches concrete and leaves visible wand marks.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finish with a rinse using a wide tip to push dirty water and loosened grit off the slab. Edges and corners the surface cleaner cannot reach need a quick pass with the wand. Our step-by-step guide to <a href="/how-to-pressure-wash-a-patio/">pressure washing a patio</a> covers pavers and stone as well as concrete, which need gentler treatment.
          </p>

          <p>
            Be cautious on new concrete, which should cure for some weeks before pressure washing, and on old, crumbling or exposed-aggregate concrete, which can lose surface paste or aggregate under high pressure. Test a hidden spot first and back off the pressure or widen the tip if you see the surface roughening.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="stains" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Oil, Rust and Organic Stains
          </h2>

          <p>
            A pressure washer removes surface dirt, mildew and loose grime well, but it does not lift every stain. Oil and grease soak into the concrete's pores, and water alone tends to spread them rather than remove them. Treat oil spots with a degreaser or poultice before washing; our guide to <a href="/how-to-get-oil-out-of-concrete/">getting oil out of concrete</a> covers the methods by stain age.
          </p>

          <p>
            Rust stains from furniture, fertilizer or irrigation water need a rust remover formulated for concrete, used according to the label, because pressure alone does little. Organic stains from leaves, algae and mildew respond well to an appropriate cleaner followed by washing. Tire marks, paint and sealer residue each need their own approach.
          </p>

          <p>
            Hot water helps with grease, but most homeowner washers are cold-water units, and most manufacturers say not to feed them hot water. Our explainer on <a href="/can-you-put-hot-water-in-a-pressure-washer/">putting hot water in a pressure washer</a> covers why. For heavy grease, a good degreaser and longer dwell time is the practical route.
          </p>

          <p>
            Expect some staining to remain even after a thorough clean. Concrete is porous, and deep or old stains can be lightened but not always removed. Sealing afterwards is what keeps new stains from soaking in so deeply.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="sealing-after" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. After Cleaning: Drying and Sealing
          </h2>

          <p>
            Cleaning is often the first step of a bigger job. If you plan to seal the driveway or patio, a thorough pressure wash is the preparation every sealer label asks for, because sealer will not bond to dirt, oil or loose material. Our guide to <a href="/pressure-wash-driveway-before-sealing/">pressure washing a driveway before sealing</a> covers the steps and timing.
          </p>

          <p>
            Let the concrete dry fully before sealing. Sealer manufacturers generally specify a dry surface, and many recommend waiting at least a day after washing, longer in cool or humid weather. Sealing over damp concrete can cause clouding and poor adhesion, which shows up as a white haze or peeling.
          </p>

          <p>
            Choose the sealer for the surface and the look you want. Penetrating sealers soak in and leave the surface looking natural, while film-forming acrylics add sheen and color depth. Our <a href="/best-concrete-sealer/">concrete sealer guide</a> compares them. For asphalt, the process is different, and our <a href="/best-asphalt-driveway-sealer-reviews/">asphalt driveway sealer guide</a> covers it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Clean and inspect the washer after each concrete session. Grit picked up from the slab can wear nozzles and surface cleaner seals, and a worn nozzle widens the spray and reduces cleaning power. Replacing nozzles is cheap, and doing it when the pattern starts to look uneven keeps the results consistent.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using the 0-degree tip on concrete</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A pencil-thin jet concentrates all the pressure on a tiny spot and can etch or gouge concrete, especially older slabs. Use a 25 or 40-degree tip for rinsing and a surface cleaner for the main cleaning.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying on peak PSI alone</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Flow does as much of the work on a driveway as pressure. Compare gallons per minute at rated pressure, not peak figures that occur at different times, and favor machines around 2.5 GPM or more for driveways.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Pairing an oversized surface cleaner</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A deck too large for the washer's flow spins slowly and leaves swirls and stripes. Match the deck to the washer's PSI and GPM, typically 15 to 16 inches for homeowner gas machines and 12 to 14 for electrics.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the detergent</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Water alone struggles with oil, mildew and organic staining, and turning up the pressure risks damaging the surface. Pre-treat with a concrete cleaner or degreaser and let it dwell before washing.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing before the concrete dries</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Sealer applied to damp concrete can cloud, whiten or peel. Let the slab dry fully, typically at least a day after washing, before applying any sealer.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

