        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best pressure washer for concrete?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most driveways, the Simpson PowerShot PS3228, listed at 3,300 PSI and 2.5 GPM with a Honda GX200 and AAA triplex pump. On a budget, the Westinghouse WPX3200 is rated 3,200 PSI and 2.5 GPM. For large areas, the DeWalt DXPW4440 is rated 4,400 PSI and 4.0 GPM.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What PSI do you need to clean concrete?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Around 3,000 PSI is a good target for driveways, paired with 2.5 GPM or more of flow. Lower-pressure electric washers can clean patios and walks, more slowly, especially with a small surface cleaner and detergent. More pressure than you need increases the risk of etching.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a gas or electric pressure washer better for concrete?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Gas, for most driveways. Gas washers here offer roughly twice the flow of electrics at higher pressure, so they finish faster and run larger surface cleaners. Electric makes sense for patios, walks and small areas, or where quiet, low-maintenance operation matters more than speed.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need a surface cleaner for concrete?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not strictly, but it is the biggest upgrade you can make. A rotary surface cleaner cleans an even path without wand stripes, works faster and keeps spray contained. Size it to the washer: around 15 to 16 inches for typical gas washers and 12 to 14 for electrics.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a pressure washer damage concrete?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, with narrow tips, too much pressure, or lingering in one spot. Older, soft or exposed-aggregate concrete is most at risk. Use wide tips or a surface cleaner, keep moving, test a hidden area first, and avoid washing new concrete before it has cured.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will a pressure washer remove oil stains from concrete?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not on its own. Oil soaks into concrete's pores, so treat stains with a degreaser or poultice first, let it dwell, then wash. Old or deep oil stains may lighten rather than disappear completely.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How often should you pressure wash a driveway?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Many homeowners clean once a year, often in spring or before sealing. Shady, damp driveways that grow mildew or algae may need it more often. Sealing after cleaning makes future cleaning easier and reduces how deeply stains soak in.
              </p>
            </div>

